// diag_pos_expr_reject: a diagnostic anchored on an EXPRESSION node must
// land on the line of that expression, not on the line of the next token.
//
// Batch 1 (PR #221) moved statement and declaration nodes onto bison's @$
// locations, but left the expression and literal constructors on
// get_current_position() — which returns the LIVE lexer cursor, i.e. the
// LOOKAHEAD token bison has already read. "constant N overflows T" is
// positioned by check_literal_range (expression_checker.c) from the LITERAL
// node, so it kept pointing one token past the literal: this fixture's error
// was reported on line 21 (`_ = x`) instead of line 20.
//
// The sidecar pins the LINE only, deliberately. The COLUMN is still one too
// large across the whole compiler — lexer_new sets pos.column = 1 and then
// immediately calls lexer_read_char, which increments it — and that
// off-by-one is ledgered as its own arc in .handoff.md (a naive fix yields
// column 0 on the ASI path). Go reports column 15 here, pointing at `200`.
package main

func main() {
	var x int8 = 200
	_ = x
}
